---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Chainsaws",
    "url": "/best-chainsaw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Log Splitters",
    "url": "/best-log-splitter/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Leaf Vacuums",
    "url": "/best-leaf-vacuum/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Pole Saws",
    "url": "/best-pole-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Hedge Trimmers",
    "url": "/best-hedge-trimmer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Leaf Blowers",
    "url": "/best-leaf-blower/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-19", modified: "2026-10-19" });
const title = "7 Best Wood Chippers: Electric, Gas and PTO Picks for Every Yard";
const description =
  "The best wood chippers for pruning, wooded lots and acreage, from electric to gas chipper-shredders and PTO models, compared by capacity, engine and weight.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "earthquake-k33-chipper-shredder",
    "name": "Earthquake K33 Chipper Shredder (301cc Viper)",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Gas Chipper-Shredder, 3-Inch Capacity",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Earthquake K33 is the chipper we would point most homeowners with a wooded lot toward, because it pairs a 3-inch rating with more engine than most machines in its class. Retailers including Tractor Supply and Farm and Fleet list a 301cc Viper four-cycle engine, and Farm and Fleet lists torque of 13.28 ft-lb at 2,500 RPM, a figure Bob Vila's review repeats. That torque is what keeps the rotor turning when you feed a green 2-inch limb, which is where cheaper 212cc machines tend to bog. Small material goes into a separate hopper, where Bob Vila describes two J-hammers and two tri-hammers shredding leaves and twigs. It rolls on two 11-inch airless wheels, so a flat tire is not a spring-cleanup surprise. The trade-off is bulk: listed weights range from about 138 to 140 pounds net, and one seller lists 161 pounds assembled, so this is a machine you push to the brush pile rather than carry. Novawurks lists a five-year limited warranty; confirm the term with the seller you buy from.",
    "pros": [
      "301cc Viper engine with 13.28 ft-lb of torque listed by Farm and Fleet",
      "Rated for branches up to 3 inches",
      "Separate hopper with J-hammers and tri-hammers for leaves and twigs",
      "11-inch airless wheels cannot go flat",
      "Five-year limited warranty listed by one retailer"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Roughly 138-161 lb depending on the listing, so not easy to move on slopes",
      "Recoil start only, no electric start",
      "Oil capacity is listed at about 37 oz, more than the bottle included per Bob Vila"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a homeowner clearing storm limbs and pruning from a wooded lot, the Earthquake K33 has the engine to make its 3-inch rating realistic on the branches most yards actually produce."
  },
  {
    "id": "dr-premier-300-chipper-shredder",
    "name": "DR Premier 300 Chipper Shredder",
    "badge": "Best for Frequent Use",
    "category": "Gas Chipper-Shredder, 3-Inch Capacity",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "DR has built chipper shredders for long enough that parts and dealer support are part of what you are buying, and the Premier 300 is its mainstream 3-inch model. DR's listing gives a 3-inch chipping capacity, a 7.25 by 7.5-inch chipper hopper and a hardened chipper knife, with shredding up to half an inch through eight free-swinging hammers and a stated 10:1 reduction. Power comes from a Briggs and Stratton OHV engine of about 208cc and 6.5 HP with a manual start. DR lists the weight at about 126 pounds. On paper the engine is smaller than the Earthquake's, so the reason to choose it is ownership rather than raw power: DR sells replacement knives and hammers directly and has a long record with this design. Pricing moves around constantly; DR has listed it with a list price near $970 and frequent sale prices well below that, and its later Premier 320 adds a 223cc engine and a larger 10.4 by 7.2-inch chipper opening. Check which version you are being quoted before comparing.",
    "pros": [
      "3-inch chipping capacity and 1/2-inch shredding listed by DR",
      "Eight free-swinging shredder hammers with a stated 10:1 reduction",
      "Briggs and Stratton OHV engine",
      "Replacement knives and hammers sold directly by DR",
      "Premier 320 version adds a bigger engine and opening"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 6.5 HP is less engine than the Earthquake K33",
      "Frequent price changes make the real cost hard to pin down",
      "About 126 lb listed weight"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The DR Premier 300 is the pick for someone who will chip every season for years and values parts availability and a long-running design over the biggest engine."
  },
  {
    "id": "champion-200905-chipper-shredder",
    "name": "Champion 200905 3-Inch Portable Chipper Shredder",
    "badge": "Best Value Gas",
    "category": "Gas Chipper-Shredder, 3-Inch Capacity",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Champion 200905 is the gas chipper to buy when you want a real 3-inch machine without paying DR prices. Champion's copy describes a 224cc single-cylinder OHV engine with a 0.92-gallon fuel tank, and the chipper chute is rated for branches up to 3 inches while the shredder handles material up to about half an inch. A Champion sell sheet hosted by Zoro lists two hardened steel chipper blades, a 15.2-inch shredder blade with six shredder hammers, a 2-bushel collection bag and a weight of about 114 pounds. That sell sheet also lists a 20:1 reduction ratio, though an independent spec site notes Champion does not publish that figure prominently and it appears to derive from the company's claim of turning 20 bags of debris into one. The collection bag is a real convenience for small yards where you want mulch in one place rather than sprayed across the lawn. The engine sits between budget 212cc machines and the Earthquake's 301cc, which is about where its realistic capacity sits too.",
    "pros": [
      "224cc OHV engine listed by Champion",
      "3-inch chipper and 1/2-inch shredder capacity",
      "Two-bushel collection bag included",
      "About 114 lb, lighter than the DR and Earthquake",
      "Champion has a wide US service and parts network"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The 20:1 reduction figure is a marketing-derived number",
      "Less torque in hard, green wood than larger engines",
      "Collection bag fills quickly when chipping steadily"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Champion 200905 is the best value in a full 3-inch gas chipper-shredder, with enough engine for typical pruning and a bag that keeps the mulch tidy."
  },
  {
    "id": "superhandy-7hp-mini-wood-chipper",
    "name": "SuperHandy 7HP Mini Wood Chipper Shredder",
    "badge": "Best Budget Gas",
    "category": "Gas Chipper, 3-Inch Rating",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The SuperHandy 7HP is the cheapest way into a gas chipper that does not depend on an extension cord, and it is honest about being compact. Listings at Lowe's and Woot describe a 212cc, 7 HP four-stroke OHV engine with a recoil start, 9.14 ft-lb of torque at 2,500 RPM, two CR12MoV cutting blades, a 15:1 reduction ratio and a 0.7-gallon fuel tank, with SuperHandy recommending ethanol-free fuel. At a listed 85 pounds it is light enough to load into a pickup bed with two people, and its vertical discharge chute is adjustable, although listings disagree on whether the range is 90 to 135 or 90 to 145 degrees. Treat the 3-inch rating as a best case. A buyer review on Woot's forum reports the machine struggling with 2-inch wood and stalling, and that is consistent with what a 212cc engine can do against a dense, green branch. For small limbs, prunings and an occasional storm cleanup it is reasonable value.",
    "pros": [
      "Low price for a gas chipper",
      "212cc OHV engine with 9.14 ft-lb of torque listed",
      "85 lb listed weight",
      "15:1 reduction ratio listed",
      "Adjustable discharge chute"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Owners report bogging on 2-inch wood, well below the rating",
      "Recoil start only",
      "One-year limited warranty listed by Woot"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The SuperHandy 7HP is a sensible budget choice for small limbs and pruning, provided you plan around about 2 inches rather than the 3 on the box."
  },
  {
    "id": "sun-joe-cj603e-electric-chipper",
    "name": "Sun Joe CJ603E 15-Amp Electric Wood Chipper Shredder",
    "badge": "Best Electric",
    "category": "Electric Chipper-Shredder, 1.73-Inch Capacity",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "For a suburban lot where the job is pruning rather than storm cleanup, the Sun Joe CJ603E does the work without fuel, oil or a pull cord. Sun Joe's listings give a 15-amp motor, a cutting capacity of branches up to 1.73 inches thick and a 21:1 reduction ratio, and retailers describe a safety hopper that stops the motor when it is opened. It rolls on 7-inch wheels, and one review lists the weight at about 38.6 pounds, roughly a third of a gas chipper's. Sun Joe markets it as a quiet machine, but quiet is relative: one review lists a noise rating of 94 dB, so ear protection is still sensible. The capacity figure deserves attention, because a closely related model, the CJ602E, is listed at 1.5 inches, and the two are easy to confuse on marketplace listings. Sun Joe lists a two-year warranty. If your brush is mostly shrub prunings and limbs thinner than a broom handle, this is the machine that will get used rather than stored.",
    "pros": [
      "No gas, oil or pull starting",
      "1.73-inch capacity and 21:1 reduction listed by Sun Joe",
      "About 38.6 lb per one review, easy to move and store",
      "Safety hopper cuts power when opened",
      "Two-year warranty listed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Far smaller capacity than any gas pick",
      "Needs a heavy-gauge extension cord and an outlet nearby",
      "Easy to confuse with the 1.5-inch CJ602E"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Sun Joe CJ603E is the right chipper for pruning-sized material on a typical suburban lot, with none of the upkeep of a gas engine."
  },
  {
    "id": "patriot-csv-3100b-chipper",
    "name": "Patriot Products CSV-3100B Wood Chipper Leaf Shredder",
    "badge": "Best Heavy-Duty",
    "category": "Gas Chipper-Shredder, Direct Drive, 3-Inch Capacity",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Patriot CSV-3100B is for property owners who chip every week of the season and have broken lighter machines doing it. Retailer listings describe a 10 HP Briggs and Stratton Vanguard commercial-grade engine, a 20-pound rotor carrying two chipping knives and six shredding Y-hammers, and a maximum branch diameter of 3 inches. Its defining feature is a direct drive with no belts or pulleys, which removes the belt slipping and replacement that wear down belt-driven chippers. The capacity is the same 3 inches as cheaper machines, so the money buys duty cycle and durability rather than bigger branches. One retailer lists it at about $1,825, which puts it in a different class from everything else here, and I could not find an official Patriot spec sheet to confirm the full figures, so check them against the dealer's listing. If you need more than 3 inches, a tow-behind or PTO chipper is the next step rather than a bigger walk-behind.",
    "pros": [
      "10 HP Briggs and Stratton Vanguard commercial engine listed",
      "Direct drive with no belts or pulleys",
      "Heavy 20 lb rotor with two knives and six Y-hammers",
      "Built for frequent, sustained use"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About $1,825 at one retailer, far above other walk-behind picks",
      "No larger branch capacity than cheaper 3-inch machines",
      "Official spec sheet not found; confirm figures with the dealer"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Patriot CSV-3100B earns its price only if you chip constantly; for that owner, the commercial engine and beltless drive are the point."
  },
  {
    "id": "woodland-mills-wc68-pto-chipper",
    "name": "Woodland Mills WC68 6-Inch PTO Wood Chipper",
    "badge": "Best PTO Chipper",
    "category": "3-Point PTO Chipper, 6-Inch Capacity, Hydraulic Feed",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "If you own a compact tractor, a PTO chipper changes the job entirely, and the Woodland Mills WC68 is a well-specified example. Woodland Mills lists it for tractors with 20 to 50 HP at the PTO, with a 6-inch capacity through a 6 by 8-inch opening, an automatic hydraulic infeed, direct drive and a 360-degree rotating discharge chute. The flywheel is listed at 24 inches across and three-quarters of an inch thick, turning at the standard 540 RPM, and the machine mounts on the tractor's three-point hitch. Hydraulic infeed matters more than the capacity figure: rollers pull branches in at a controlled rate, so you are not wrestling limbs into a knife. Woodland Mills's EU store lists it at €3,290, and the US page mentions a three-year warranty and flat-rate shipping; check the US price directly. Remember that a 6-inch rating assumes cooperative softwood, and dense hardwood will slow it down.",
    "pros": [
      "6-inch capacity with a 6 x 8-inch opening",
      "Automatic hydraulic infeed",
      "Fits 20-50 HP tractors on a three-point hitch",
      "360-degree rotating discharge chute",
      "Three-year warranty listed on the US page"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Requires a compact tractor with a 540 RPM PTO",
      "US price not shown on the page found; confirm with Woodland Mills",
      "Hardwood near the rated size will slow feeding"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For acreage owners with a compact tractor, the Woodland Mills WC68 handles limbs no walk-behind chipper can, with hydraulic feed that makes the work safer and faster."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Wood Chippers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-wood-chipper/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size wood chipper do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Match the chipper to the branches you produce regularly, not the biggest one you might see. Electric chippers suit material under about 1.5 inches, gas walk-behind chipper-shredders handle around 2 inches comfortably with a 3-inch rating, and PTO chippers on compact tractors take 5 inches or more. Plan around two-thirds of the rated capacity for green hardwood."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are electric wood chippers any good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for the right job. An electric chipper like the Sun Joe CJ603E handles pruning-sized material up to about 1.7 inches with no fuel, oil or pull starting, and weighs around 40 pounds. It is not suited to storm cleanup or mature trees, where a gas chipper's larger capacity and cordless operation matter."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a good reduction ratio for a wood chipper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most homeowner machines are quoted between 10:1 and 20:1, meaning ten to twenty bags of brush become one bag of chips. Treat the figure as marketing rather than a measured specification, since results depend heavily on the material. Higher ratios generally produce finer mulch; lower ratios produce coarser chips."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a wood chipper handle leaves?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A chipper-shredder can, through its shredder hopper, which uses swinging hammers to turn leaves and twigs into mulch. The narrow chipper chute is for branches only, and leaves fed into it tend to wrap the rotor. If leaves are most of your volume, a leaf vacuum or mulcher does that job better."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I keep a wood chipper from jamming?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Feed steadily and let the machine pull material in at its own pace, rather than forcing in a large bundle. Keep leafy material out of the chipper chute, keep the knife sharp, and trim side branches off limbs so they feed straight. Always stop the engine and wait for the rotor to stop before clearing a jam."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a PTO wood chipper worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you already own a compact tractor with enough PTO horsepower, typically 20 HP or more for a 6-inch machine, a PTO chipper is the most capable option for acreage, with larger capacity and hydraulic feed. Without the tractor, it is not a sensible purchase, because the tractor is the expensive part."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What safety gear do I need for a wood chipper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers consistently recommend safety glasses or a face shield, hearing protection, heavy gloves, and close-fitting clothing with no loose sleeves, drawstrings or jewelry. Stand to the side of the feed chute, keep children and pets away, and never reach into the chute while the machine is running."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best wood chipper",
      "wood chipper shredder",
      "electric wood chipper",
      "gas wood chipper",
      "pto wood chipper",
      "chipper shredder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Wood Chippers <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">for Every Yard</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Electric chippers for pruning, gas chipper-shredders for wooded lots and PTO chippers for acreage, sized by what each can really chew through.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#earthquake-k33-chipper-shredder"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#chipper-vs-shredder" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Chipper vs Shredder</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#capacity" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Branch Capacity</a>
          <a href="#gas-vs-electric-vs-pto" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Gas, Electric or PTO</a>
          <a href="#reduction-ratio" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Reduction Ratio</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Chipper Safety</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#what-to-do-with-chips" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Using the Chips</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Which to Buy</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best wood chipper is the one sized to the branches your property actually produces, not the biggest number on a box. Every chipper's capacity is a best case on soft, straight wood, and the gap between a 3-inch rating on a small engine and the same rating on a large one is the difference between a machine that chips and one that stalls. This guide sorts the field into electric, gas walk-behind and PTO machines, and tells you which one fits your yard.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these chippers. We compared manufacturer specifications, dealer and retailer listings, owner manuals and independent reviews, and credited each figure to its source in the product write-ups. Where sources disagreed, which happened often with weights, capacities and reduction ratios, we gave the range or flagged the conflict rather than picking the most flattering number.
          </p>

          <p>
            The shortlist covers the three ways homeowners actually chip: electric machines for pruning on small lots, gas walk-behind chipper-shredders for typical wooded yards, and PTO chippers for owners with a compact tractor and real acreage. Within each group we favoured machines with a published engine or motor specification, a stated branch capacity, and a brand with a parts and service network you can reach after the warranty runs out.
          </p>

          <p>
            We gave the most weight to engine displacement and torque, because they decide whether a machine can actually chip a branch near its rated size. A 3-inch rating on a 212cc machine and a 3-inch rating on a 301cc machine are not the same promise. We also weighed weight and wheels, since a chipper you cannot move to the brush pile does not get used, and we noted warranty terms only where a seller or manufacturer published them.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out machines whose specifications conflicted so badly across listings that we could not tell which model number a figure belonged to. That is a real problem in this category: several brands sell near-identical models under different numbers with different capacities, and a chipper bought on the wrong number is a frustrating return. Every pick here has a model you can match on the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="chipper-vs-shredder" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Chipper, Shredder or Both
          </h2>

          <p>
            A wood chipper and a shredder do different jobs, and most homeowner machines combine both. The chipper side has a narrow chute that feeds branches into a spinning blade or knife, which slices them into chips. It handles the woody material: limbs, saplings and prunings up to the rated diameter. The shredder side has a wide hopper above a set of swinging hammers that batter soft material into mulch, which is what you want for leaves, twigs and garden waste.
          </p>

          <p>
            Combination chipper-shredders like the Earthquake K33, DR Premier 300 and Champion 200905 put both on one engine. That is the right choice for most yards, because spring and fall cleanups produce a mix of branches and leaves. The shredder capacity is usually small, around half an inch, so it is for twigs and leaves rather than anything you would call a branch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dedicated chippers skip the hopper and put everything into one heavier rotor or flywheel. PTO chippers like the Woodland Mills WC68 work this way, and so do many tow-behind machines. They take much larger branches and produce cleaner chips, but they are poor at leaves. If your main problem is leaves, a dedicated leaf mulcher or vacuum does that job better than any chipper; our guide to the <a href="/best-leaf-vacuum/">best leaf vacuums</a> covers those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric units like the Sun Joe CJ603E are usually chipper-shredders in a small format, with a single feed chute that handles both thin branches and leafy prunings. For a suburban garden where the material is shrub cuttings and small limbs, that single-chute design is perfectly adequate and much easier to live with.
          </p>
        </div>

