        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Branch Capacity Is a Best Case
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every chipper is sold on its maximum branch diameter, and every one of those numbers is a best case. Manufacturers rate capacity on cooperative wood: straight, relatively soft and often dry. Green hardwood, knotty limbs and forked branches are much harder to chip, and a machine will often bog well before its rated size on that material. One SuperHandy owner reports stalling on 2-inch wood with a machine rated for 3.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful rule is to plan around two-thirds of the rated diameter for regular work, and to reserve the full rating for the occasional straight softwood limb. That puts a 3-inch machine in the 2-inch range for everyday chipping and a 6-inch PTO chipper in the 4-inch range for hardwood. If your property produces lots of limbs near the top of a machine's rating, buy the next size up rather than hoping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Engine size and torque are what make the rating realistic. The Earthquake K33's 301cc engine and listed 13.28 ft-lb of torque give it more headroom at 3 inches than a 212cc machine with about 9 ft-lb. Electric chippers are a separate scale entirely: a 15-amp motor is limited by the household circuit, which is why the Sun Joe tops out around 1.7 inches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Branches larger than your chipper's realistic size do not have to be wasted. Cut them to firewood length with a chainsaw instead; our <a href="/best-chainsaw/">chainsaw guide</a> covers the sizes that suit homeowners, and anything over about 6 inches is usually better split than chipped, which our <a href="/best-log-splitter/">log splitter guide</a> covers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-electric-vs-pto" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gas, Electric or PTO
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric chippers suit small lots and pruning work. They start at the push of a button, weigh around 40 pounds, need no fuel or oil changes, and are quiet compared with gas. Their limits are capacity, usually under 2 inches, and the cord: they need an outlet nearby and a heavy-gauge extension cord sized for a 15-amp load, or they lose power and can overheat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas walk-behind chipper-shredders are the middle ground and the right choice for most homeowners with trees. A 200cc to 300cc engine handles 3-inch branches on paper and around 2 inches comfortably, and the machine can go anywhere you can push it. They need the same care as any small engine: fresh fuel, oil changes and winter storage. Expect them to weigh 85 to 160 pounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            PTO chippers are for owners of compact tractors with real acreage or woodland. They draw power from the tractor's power take-off, so the capacity can reach 5 to 8 inches, and hydraulic infeed rollers pull material in at a controlled rate. The catch is that you need the tractor, typically 20 HP or more at the PTO for a 6-inch machine, and the chipper costs several thousand dollars on top of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tow-behind chippers with their own engine are a fourth option for large properties without a tractor, but they are priced and sized for frequent use. For most readers, the decision comes down to the size of the material and whether you already own a tractor: under 1.5 inches, electric; up to about 2.5 inches in practice, gas walk-behind; larger, PTO.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reduction-ratio" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Reduction Ratio and What It Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reduction ratio describes how much the machine shrinks the volume of what goes in. A 10:1 ratio means ten bags of loose brush become one bag of chips or mulch. Manufacturers quote figures from 10:1 to over 20:1, and the numbers in this guide range from DR's stated 10:1 on the Premier 300 to Sun Joe's listed 21:1 on the CJ603E.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat reduction ratios as marketing figures rather than specifications. There is no standard test behind them, and the result depends heavily on the material: dry leaves compress far more than green branches. The Champion's 20:1 figure, for example, appears to come from the company's claim that it turns 20 bags of debris into one, rather than from a measured test.
          </p>

          <p>
            What reduction ratio does tell you is how fine the output is. Higher ratios come from more hammers, smaller screens or more cuts per revolution, and they produce mulch you can spread straight onto beds. Lower ratios produce coarser chips that are better for paths and composting. Neither is better in the abstract; match it to how you plan to use the output.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to compost the output, coarser chips break down more slowly but keep the pile aerated, while fine shredded leaves break down fast but can mat. Mixing the two is ideal, and a chipper-shredder that does both is a good fit for a gardener who wants to keep yard waste on the property instead of bagging it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Chipper Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood chippers are among the most dangerous tools homeowners own, and almost every serious injury comes from the same few mistakes. Manufacturers' manuals are consistent on the basics: never reach into the chute or hopper while the machine is running, feed branches butt end first, let go once the machine grabs them, and use a push stick for short material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dress for the job. Safety glasses or a face shield, hearing protection and heavy gloves are standard advice from every manufacturer, and so are close-fitting sleeves with no loose cuffs, drawstrings or jewelry. A branch that catches a sleeve can pull an arm toward the rotor faster than anyone can react, which is why loose clothing is singled out in every manual.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stand to the side of the feed chute rather than directly behind it, so that a branch that kicks or whips does not hit you. Keep children, pets and helpers well away from both the feed side and the discharge, because chips can leave the chute with real force. Point the discharge away from people, windows, cars and the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn the engine off and wait for the rotor to stop completely before clearing a jam, and disconnect the spark plug wire on a gas machine or unplug an electric one before reaching inside. Most jams come from forcing in too much at once or feeding leafy material into the chipper chute; feed steadily and let the machine set the pace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance and Blade Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sharp knives are the single biggest factor in how well a chipper performs. A dull blade crushes rather than cuts, which loads the engine, slows feeding and makes the machine grab and jerk branches. Manufacturers recommend inspecting the chipper knife regularly and sharpening or replacing it when the edge is rounded or chipped, and DR sells replacement knives and hammers directly for that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most knives can be reversed or sharpened a few times before they need replacing. Sharpening should keep the original bevel angle and remove equal amounts across the edge so the blade stays balanced. Hitting a stone, nail or wire is the quickest way to ruin a knife, so check branches from fence lines and storm debris before feeding them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas engines need the same care as a mower: fresh fuel, ideally ethanol-free or stabilized, regular oil changes and a clean air filter. Chipping is dusty work, so the air filter needs checking more often than on a mower. Before storage, run the machine dry or add stabilizer, clean out the hopper and chute, and store it under cover so the rotor housing does not rust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check belts, bearings and fasteners at the start of each season. Vibration loosens bolts over time, and a belt-driven machine will lose power through a slipping belt long before it breaks. Direct-drive machines like the Patriot CSV-3100B avoid that wear item entirely, which is part of why they cost more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-do-with-chips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What to Do With the Chips
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood chips are a resource rather than waste. Spread 2 to 4 inches deep, they make a weed-suppressing mulch for beds, around trees and on garden paths, and they break down slowly into organic matter. Keep chip mulch a few inches away from tree trunks and plant stems so it does not hold moisture against the bark.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fresh chips from green wood tie up some nitrogen at the surface as they decompose, which is why some gardeners age them for a season before using them around vegetables. On paths and around established shrubs, fresh chips are fine. Shredded leaves from the hopper make an excellent compost ingredient, especially mixed with green material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chips from diseased trees are best kept off garden beds and away from healthy trees of the same species, since some diseases can survive in the wood. Chips from black walnut contain juglone, which can affect sensitive plants, so keep walnut chips for paths. If you are unsure, compost the chips fully before spreading them.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you produce more chips than you can use, local community gardens and neighbors with large beds are often glad to take them. Chipping on site also removes the trip to a green waste facility, which on its own can save hours over a season of storm cleanup and pruning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Chipper Should You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical suburban lot where the brush is mostly shrub prunings and small limbs, buy an electric chipper like the Sun Joe CJ603E. It handles material up to about the thickness of a broom handle, needs no engine maintenance, and is light enough to store on a shelf. Anything larger can go to the curb or be cut for firewood.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a wooded lot or a property with mature trees, a gas chipper-shredder is the right tool. The Earthquake K33 gives the most engine for a realistic 3-inch rating, the DR Premier 300 suits owners who value parts support and a long-running design, the Champion 200905 offers the best value in a full-size machine, and the SuperHandy 7HP is a budget choice if you plan around 2-inch wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            For acreage and an existing compact tractor, a PTO chipper like the Woodland Mills WC68 makes the most sense, with hydraulic feed and 6-inch capacity. For constant use on a large property without a tractor, the Patriot CSV-3100B is the walk-behind built for the duty cycle, though it costs as much as a small tractor implement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, pair it with the other tools the job needs. A <a href="/best-pole-saw/">pole saw</a> gets limbs down, a <a href="/best-hedge-trimmer/">hedge trimmer</a> produces the prunings, and a chainsaw handles anything too big to chip. Size the chipper to the material you actually produce and it will earn its place every spring and fall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the Box Capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Branch capacity is a best case on soft, straight wood. Plan around two-thirds of the rating for green hardwood and forked limbs, and buy the next size up if your property regularly produces branches near the limit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feeding Leaves Into the Chipper Chute</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The narrow chipper chute is for woody branches. Leaves and soft material wrap the rotor and jam it. Use the shredder hopper for leaves and twigs, or a dedicated leaf mulcher if leaves are most of your volume.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wearing Loose Clothing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufacturers warn against loose sleeves, drawstrings and jewelry because a branch can catch them and pull an arm toward the rotor. Wear close-fitting clothing, heavy gloves, eye and hearing protection, and stand to the side of the chute.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Dull Knives</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull knife crushes instead of cutting, which overloads the engine and makes the machine jerk branches. Inspect, sharpen or replace the knife regularly, and keep stones, nails and wire out of the feed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Thin Extension Cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Electric chippers draw a full 15 amps. A long or light-gauge cord starves the motor of voltage, cutting power and risking overheating. Use the heaviest-gauge, shortest cord that reaches, as the manual specifies.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

