        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size wood chipper do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the chipper to the branches you produce regularly, not the biggest one you might see. Electric chippers suit material under about 1.5 inches, gas walk-behind chipper-shredders handle around 2 inches comfortably with a 3-inch rating, and PTO chippers on compact tractors take 5 inches or more. Plan around two-thirds of the rated capacity for green hardwood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric wood chippers any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for the right job. An electric chipper like the Sun Joe CJ603E handles pruning-sized material up to about 1.7 inches with no fuel, oil or pull starting, and weighs around 40 pounds. It is not suited to storm cleanup or mature trees, where a gas chipper's larger capacity and cordless operation matter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good reduction ratio for a wood chipper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most homeowner machines are quoted between 10:1 and 20:1, meaning ten to twenty bags of brush become one bag of chips. Treat the figure as marketing rather than a measured specification, since results depend heavily on the material. Higher ratios generally produce finer mulch; lower ratios produce coarser chips.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a wood chipper handle leaves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A chipper-shredder can, through its shredder hopper, which uses swinging hammers to turn leaves and twigs into mulch. The narrow chipper chute is for branches only, and leaves fed into it tend to wrap the rotor. If leaves are most of your volume, a leaf vacuum or mulcher does that job better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I keep a wood chipper from jamming?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Feed steadily and let the machine pull material in at its own pace, rather than forcing in a large bundle. Keep leafy material out of the chipper chute, keep the knife sharp, and trim side branches off limbs so they feed straight. Always stop the engine and wait for the rotor to stop before clearing a jam.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a PTO wood chipper worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you already own a compact tractor with enough PTO horsepower, typically 20 HP or more for a 6-inch machine, a PTO chipper is the most capable option for acreage, with larger capacity and hydraulic feed. Without the tractor, it is not a sensible purchase, because the tractor is the expensive part.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What safety gear do I need for a wood chipper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers consistently recommend safety glasses or a face shield, hearing protection, heavy gloves, and close-fitting clothing with no loose sleeves, drawstrings or jewelry. Stand to the side of the feed chute, keep children and pets away, and never reach into the chute while the machine is running.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

